# Tab Grab

Tab Grab is a Chrome extension, that is my first ever chrome extension and I wanted to make it so that it could be used as a better book marker. Reducing manual labor and making sure that tabs that want to be saved are ai organized making it easier for users to go back to their tabs, and adding summary features that also help users organize their tabs better, with larger UI space compared to book marking and grouping tabs.

## Features

- Save tabs to a dedicated dashboard
- AI-powered tab organization
- AI-generated summaries
- Search saved tabs
- Organize tabs into folders
- Automatically close and save tabs after a timer

## How to Use

1. Open a tab you want to save.
2. Right-click the tab.
3. Select "Send To Dashboard."
4. Tab Grab summarizes and organizes the tab.
5. Open the Tab Grab dashboard to view your saved content.

## Built With

- JavaScript
- HTML
- CSS
- Chrome Extension Manifest V3
- Node.js
- Express
- Google Gemini API
- Chrome Storage API
- Chrome Notifications API

## How It Works

When a user clicks Send to Dashboard, it firsts stores the tab using chrome.storage.local and then using the gemini API it inputs the tab data to return a topic which is what folder the tab is going to go to. It is able to run this API call through using a fetch call. Same can be done for the AI tab summary.

## Motivation

I built tab grab cause as I worked through things getting interested in learning new things, I always opened many videos, articles but never had the attention span to get back to them, resulting in large tab spaces. I ultimately tried doing bookmarking but that took lots of my time. I wanted to create a small extension experimenting with ideas I've had over the years and seeing what I can implement within a couple of weeks. I wanted tab grab to use as little user labor or input as possible.

## Installation

### Requirements

- Google Chrome
- Node.js

### 1. Clone the repository

git clone YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL

### 2. Install backend dependencies

Open a terminal in the `server` folder and run:

npm install

### 3. Start the backend

Run:

node server.js

### 4. Load the extension

1. Open Chrome and go to `chrome://extensions/`.
2. Enable Developer Mode.
3. Click "Load unpacked."
4. Select the Tab Grab extension folder.
5. Tab Grab will now appear in Chrome.

### 5. Use Tab Grab

1. Open a tab you want to save.
2. Right-click the tab.
3. Select "Send To Dashboard."
4. Tab Grab organizes and summarizes the tab using AI.
5. Open the Tab Grab dashboard to view your saved content.


